@startuml correction-wfst-composition
' Why the joint search is *literally* N-dimensional: WFST composition builds a
' product automaton whose states are tuples (q1,q2), and the path weight is the
' semiring product of the per-dimension weights.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
title WFST composition — states are tuples, so the search is N-dimensional

rectangle "**T₁ · edit-distance**\nstates q₁, weight = distance" as t1 #1565C0
rectangle "**T₂ · n-gram LM**\nstates q₂, weight = −log ℙ(w│ctx)" as t2 #2E7D32

rectangle "**T = T₁ ∘ T₂**\nstates = pairs (q₁, q₂)  ← Cartesian product\npath weight  w(p) = ⊗ᵢ w(eᵢ)  (semiring product)" as comp #6A1B9A

rectangle "**decode**\nviterbi (Tropical) / nbest (Log)\nbeam pruning keeps ∏ of sizes tractable" as dec #37474F
rectangle "**ŵ** — joint-optimal correction" as out #455A64

t1 --> comp : ∘
t2 --> comp : ∘
comp --> dec
dec --> out

note right of comp
  Searching the composition is dynamic
  programming over a 2-D grid of (q₁,q₂);
  stacking Tₙ makes it an n-D grid.
  Global optimality does **not** compose
  from per-layer optima → beam / k-best,
  a Pareto-frontier phenomenon.
end note

legend bottom
  |= semiring |= ⊕ |= ⊗ |= decodes to |
  | Tropical | min | + | shortest path (edit distance) |
  | Log | logsumexp | + | max-probability path |
endlegend
@enduml
